+Most hooks are fire-and-forget observers — their return values are ignored. The exceptions include `pre_llm_call`, which can inject context, `pre_tool_call`, which can transform arguments or request approval, and `authorize_tool_call`, which can veto the final call. A policy whose decision depends on exact arguments belongs in the final authorization hook; earlier checks may see arguments that another hook or middleware later changes.
 
 All callbacks should accept `**kwargs` for forward compatibility. If a hook callback crashes, it's logged and skipped. Other hooks and the agent continue normally.
 
diff --git a/website/docs/user-guide/features/hooks.md b/website/docs/user-guide/features/hooks.md
index 59975d14e..fc92d99d6 100644
--- a/website/docs/user-guide/features/hooks.md
+++ b/website/docs/user-guide/features/hooks.md
@@ -619,6 +619,56 @@ def register(ctx):
 
 ---
 
+### `authorize_tool_call`
+
+Python plugins can register a **read-only final authorization** check. It runs
+after request hooks, argument rewrites and execution middleware, immediately
+before the agent callback or the registry/connector dispatch. It is intended for
+policies that depend on the exact final target and arguments, including external
+memory-derived policies. `pre_tool_call` keeps its existing single-fire transform
+and human-approval behavior; it cannot authorize arguments that change later.
+
+```python
+def authorize(tool_name, args, task_id="", **ids):
+    # Inspect current authenticated policy and the final arguments here.
+    # Approval must come from trusted authority, never an args["approved"] flag.
+    if policy_denies(tool_name, args, task_id, ids):
+        return {"action": "block", "message": "This exact call is not authorized."}
+    return {"action": "allow"}
+
+def register(ctx):
+    ctx.register_hook("authorize_tool_call", authorize)
+```
+
+The payload includes the same task, session, turn, request and tool-call identity
+fields as the pre-tool hook when available. Wrappers and connector batches also
+check their concrete child tools. Checks are idempotent: a call crossing multiple
+host boundaries can be checked again with the same identity. Do not perform
+side effects, prompt for approval, consume a one-shot approval, or mutate state
+in this hook. Approval issuance and atomic single-use admission require a
+separate authoritative service; this hook alone is not that service.
+
+Return `None` to abstain, `{"action": "allow"}` to allow this policy's check, or
+`{"action": "block", "message": "..."}` to veto. Any veto wins. A final hook
+cannot override an earlier veto. `approve`, `modify`, unrecognized results and
+argument mutation block execution. Arguments are detached JSON snapshots, so a
+callback retaining its input cannot change the checked arguments used by the
+tool after returning. Normal calls without a registered final policy retain
+their existing behavior.
+
+Callback exceptions, timeouts, worker-start errors and final-dispatch failures
+block with an error result and leave the conversation able to retry after
+recovery. Exception details from this hook are withheld from operational logs
+and error results. Policy-provided denial messages should likewise avoid secrets.
+Shell hooks do not implement this final policy contract.
+
+The hook controls the instrumented Hermes paths, not arbitrary code in a plugin,
+direct registry use by trusted application code, or tools invoked outside Hermes.
+It does not itself verify memory truth, authenticate an approval receipt, stop an
+already dispatched call or make remote effects transactional.
+
+---
+
 ### `post_tool_call`
 
 Fires **immediately after** every tool execution returns.
